  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected\nFriday, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over the mid-Atlantic region results in dry \nconditions today. Low pressure to the northwest in Canada \ntracks eastward, bringing a cold front over the state Friday \ninto Saturday. Pressure gradient between the high pressure and \nthe approaching low could help produce some breezy southwest \nwinds this afternoon. \n\nBased on global models, PWATs are roughly 1.25-1.75 inches, \nwith higher values concentrated around the central and Downeast \nregions. Models continue to hint at precipitation generally \nmoving in early Friday from the northwest, and moving to the \nsoutheast by Friday night. There is still some model \ndiscrepancies in the end timing, however. Global ensembles \ngenerally keep precipitation totals between half an inch to an\ninch. Some members, though, do show a narrow band of enhanced \nprecipitation, upwards of an inch, somewhere located around\nCentral Highlands and Downeast region. The area of enhanced\nprecipitation is most likely to occur should the front slow in\nprogress, arriving to the Bangor and Downeast regions during\npeak heating and allowing diurnal heating to support vertical\ndevelopment and more efficient rain mechanisms. Additionally,\nthe NAMNEST does hint at the potential for a coastal low to\nbegin to develop, further enhancing rainfall in the Bangor and\nDowneast regions into Friday evening. However, this is an\noutlier solution, and should be considered as the extreme upper\nend for precip totals. Other CAMs guidance, such as the HRRR, \nsuggest that rain could become more showery as the front \napproaches the coast, with less moisture support as the boundary\nbegins to weaken.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain is possible Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure builds in over the area this weekend. A low \npressure system from southern Canada moves eastward at the same \ntime as a low pressure exits off the Mid-Atlantic coast Monday. \nThe interaction between the high pressure and the two lows \nprovides significant uncertainty about rainfall. A few scenarios\nare currently playing out in the models. 1. The high pressure \ncould dominate forcing the lows to stay to the north and south, \nlimiting precip. 2. The Canadian low could enhance the coastal \nlow as it rides the coast north, bringing more substantial \nprecip to coastal areas. 3. The Canadian low could dominate, \nbringing enhanced precipitation along its cold front from the \nmoisture over the southeast low. \n\nAt this time, uncertainty from the models is high, so forecast \nconfidence is low, with a wide range of precipitation values.\nThe interaction of the pressure systems could also produce some\ngusty winds. Continue to monitor the forecast for updates, as \nmodels begin to get a better handle on the atmospheric set up.",
